model UserTransaction {
  id           Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  userId       String
  type         String
  amount       Int
  category     String
  description  String
  balanceAfter Int?
  metadata     Json     @default("{}")
  createdAt    DateTime @default(now())
  user         User     @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Equoria-qh6jk / Equoria-mxftz / Equoria-69gip: a SECOND index on the same
  /// two columns exists in the database —
  /// `user_transactions_user_created_idx ("userId", "createdAt" DESC)`, created
  /// in raw SQL by 20260528120000_qh6jk_align_runtime_indexes after
  /// backend/services/financialLedgerService.mjs had been creating it at
  /// runtime. It is now DECLARED below, with `map:` pinning that name, so
  /// `prisma migrate dev` no longer proposes dropping it. Nothing needs deleting
  /// from a generated migration; the earlier instruction to do that has been
  /// removed as the wrong remedy (see the `Horse` block).
  ///
  /// `sort:` has been generally available since Prisma 4.0, needs no
  /// `previewFeatures`, and this very file already uses it six times — see
  /// `Notification`, which declares the identical `(userId, createdAt DESC)`
  /// shape. `map:` pins the existing index name, the idiom `Show` uses.
  ///
  /// WITHDRAWN CLAIM (Equoria-69gip, 2026-09-09). This block previously said the
  /// DESC index "is NOT redundant with the `@@index` below: that one ascends,
  /// the raw one descends, which is what newest-first ledger reads want", and
  /// that dropping it would leave "the newest-first ledger read with a worse
  /// plan". The two index DEFINITIONS do differ, and that half is true. The
  /// load-bearing inference does not hold. This table's only reader is
  /// `getTransactionsForUser`
  /// (backend/modules/economy/services/financialLedgerService.mjs), which issues
  /// `WHERE "userId" = ? ORDER BY "createdAt" DESC, id DESC LIMIT n`. Read-only
  /// `EXPLAIN` inside `BEGIN READ ONLY` with `enable_seqscan` off plans a Bitmap
  /// Index Scan on this index for the `userId` equality followed by a Sort — the
  /// DESC ordering is never used, and the identical plan shape appears for
  /// `ORDER BY "createdAt" ASC`. No index in this schema can satisfy the full
  /// `createdAt DESC, id DESC` order, so a sort step survives either way.
  ///
  /// Caveat, stated because this ticket's history is a lesson in overclaiming:
  /// that measurement was on 239 rows. At production scale the planner could
  /// switch to an ordered index scan — though the `id DESC` tiebreaker keeps a
  /// sort or incremental sort in the plan regardless. So this is grounds to
  /// DECIDE the index deliberately (Equoria-bebob), NOT grounds to assume it is
  /// load-bearing and NOT grounds to drop it. It is declared below exactly as it
  /// exists in the database; the ASC `@@index` was deliberately NOT substituted
  /// for it, because that would change behaviour.
  ///
  /// Measured, not assumed (Equoria-mxftz fix round 2,
  /// .superpowers/sdd/FINDINGS/task-14-r2-desc-index-declarable.log, re-measured
  /// under Equoria-69gip 2026-09-09): the declaration below changes the migration
  /// Prisma generates by exactly one statement — the `DROP INDEX` disappears and
  /// NOTHING is added. Prisma treats the declaration as already satisfied by the
  /// index qh6jk created, because the name, columns and ordering all match.
  /// **So this needed no migration; it is a pure declaration catch-up.** Round 1
  /// of this task claimed a DESC ordering could not be expressed here at all.
  /// That was wrong, and `Notification` above was the disproof.
  @@index([userId, createdAt(sort: Desc)], map: "user_transactions_user_created_idx")
  @@index([userId, createdAt])
  @@index([category])
  @@map("user_transactions")
}

